# A shell script committed with CRLF has `#!/bin/sh\r` for a shebang, and a Linux kernel then looks for
# an interpreter called `/bin/sh\r` and does not find one. It fails as exit 127, "required file not
# found", which points at the script rather than at the line ending that broke it — so this is pinned
# rather than left to whatever the checking-out machine happens to do.
#
# Deliberately no `* text=auto`: that would renormalise every text file in the repo on the next
# checkout, and the problem being solved here is two files wide.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# The other half of the same rule. These are only ever run by Windows, where CRLF is what is expected.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
